<TopicPage slug="subjects/geography/intermediate/rivers-floods-and-deltas">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Rivers are <strong>planetary plumbing and sediment conveyors</strong> — and the stage on
    which most human history happened. Civilisations arose on floodplains (Nile, Tigris-
    Euphrates, Indus, Yellow River) precisely because rivers deliver water, fertile silt and
    transport. They also flood, shift and drown — the same processes that built the ground
    under the cities.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the river system, source to delta" widget="equationStep" config={riverCfg} />

  <h2>Floods: the recurring exam question</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Floodplains are rivers' territory:</strong> a "100-year flood" has a 1% chance in any year — and 26% chance over a 30-year mortgage. Building on floodplains converts natural processes into disasters.</li>
    <li><strong>Management options trade off:</strong> dams (power, flood control — but sediment starvation downstream, as the Nile's delta shows), levees (protect one bank, raise flood levels next door), and "room for the river" (Netherlands' modern approach: give the flood space).</li>
    <li><strong>Case study: Bangladesh.</strong> Deltaic, low-lying, monsoon-fed, densely populated — plus Himalayan rivers plus sea-level rise. The country that symbolised catastrophe now leads in community-based flood adaptation: warning systems, floating farms, cyclone shelters that have cut storm deaths tenfold.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> OpenStax, An Introduction to Geology (Streams); UN World Water Development Report; Bridge, Water: The Causes, Costs and Consequences of a Global Water Crisis.</p>
</TopicPage>
